1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,500
This is Diligence Room, running right now. Eight agents
doing end-to-end due diligence on an M&A deal.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,500
On the left, you see the live pipeline. On the right,
my app deployed on Cloud Run.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,500
It starts clean. Watch it fill up.

4
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:23,000
I'm Divyansh Agrawal, and this is my entry for the
Fortified Enterprise Fleet track.

5
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:27,500
I am replaying fourteen days of a real deal at high speed.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
And look at that, there they are. Findings landing live
as agents write them into Firestore.

7
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:43,000
When one company buys another, human analysts must read
every paper the seller hands over.

8
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:52,000
Thousands of files, weeks of tedious work. Things slip
through simply because nobody has time to cross-reference
all of it.

9
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Here, Gemma 4 screens every file first, and Model Armor
checks it for prompt injection.

10
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:07,000
An agent cannot post a finding unless it quotes the exact
clause it came from.

11
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:13,500
Forty-nine events processed, five findings flagged, and it
reproduces the exact same way every time.

12
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:21,000
Here is what the fleet uncovered: one critical, two high,
two medium.

13
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:31,000
That critical alert did not come from a single agent.
Legal, Finance, HR and IP each caught one piece
of the puzzle.

14
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:40,500
The target's largest customer can walk away the moment
the deal closes, taking eighteen percent of next year's
revenue with them.

15
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,500
No single specialist had the authority to call that alone.

16
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:54,000
I published this entire fleet into the Agent Registry on
Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, discoverable across
the whole company.

17
00:01:54,500 --> 00:02:01,500
All eight specialists sit here with their versions,
approval states and eval scores.

18
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,500
And Memory Bank holds everything we know about this buyer
across long-running sessions, retrieved right here
in a fresh process.

19
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:18,500
Let's look at how they work together. Legal finds a
change-of-control clause in the contract.

20
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:27,000
To size the financial risk it needs revenue data. But under
Agent Identity, Legal cannot read financial ledgers.

21
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:35,000
So it asks through the Agent Gateway. The gateway denies
access by default unless a policy allows it.

22
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:41,500
Finance returns one safe aggregate, eighteen percent,
without exposing the underlying data to the model.

23
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,500
Four workstreams pointing at the same vendor is what lets
the coordinator escalate this to critical.

24
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:55,500
I hit this fleet with twenty direct red-team attacks:
prompt injections, encoded payloads, data theft,
and cross-team writes.

25
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
The system blocked all twenty with zero false positives.

26
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:10,500
An attacker tried telling Finance the deal was already
approved. Model Armor quarantined the payload before any
agent runtime ever saw it.

27
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:19,000
What happens when an agent fails in production?
Legal runs on version 2.4. I publish version 2.5.

28
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:26,000
Our shadow harness replays a golden test set against it
and fails it: 2.5 missed that ownership clause.

29
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:34,500
I roll back to 2.4 instantly. The version reverts, but the
memory partition stays untouched. Logic and memory
stay separate.

30
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:43,000
Finally, human-in-the-loop control. The negotiation agent
drafts the request to the seller, but it cannot send it
until I approve it.

31
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:47,500
That sign-off records as a POST in Cloud Logging.

32
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
The fleet runs live on Agent Runtime and Cloud Run across
two regions, backed by Firestore and Pub/Sub.

33
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:59,000
And here is that same call in Cloud Trace, span by span,
so an auditor can follow any claim back to the source.
